course: dictionary
lesson: 485
title: Plant words: arum
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An arum is a noun, the name of a kind of plant. Its leaves are shaped like arrows, and its flower is a hood-like sheath wrapped around a spike. You can use the words around it to check this.
explain: «The titan arum, or corpse flower, from Sumatra sends up a flower spike up to about 3 metres tall.» The words «or corpse flower» are a second name for the same plant, so the titan arum is a plant, not an animal. Notice how the sentence describes its size and shape.
explain: «It smells of rotting meat to call carrion beetles and flies.» Rotting meat smells horrible, so the plant smells horrible too, on purpose. Try your meaning in the sentence: "The plant smells of rotting meat to bring in flies" still makes sense, so you have understood it.
fact: An arum is a plant with arrow-shaped leaves and a hood-like flower around a spike.
fact: The arum lily and the wild arum are both arums. The wild arum is also called cuckoo pint or lords and ladies.
fact: The wild arum's bright red-orange berries in autumn are poisonous, so never eat them.
fact: «The titan arum... smells of rotting meat, attracting beetles and flies.» Some arums smell bad to attract flies.
fact: In Australia and New Zealand, the white arum lily can grow as a weed, so it is often pulled out of gardens.
exercise: What is an arum? | A kind of bird that lives in woods | *A plant with arrow-shaped leaves and a hood-like flower | A type of beetle | A sweet red fruit you can eat
exercise: «The wild arum, also called lords and ladies, grows in woods and hedges.» Where does the wild arum grow? | Under the sea | *In woods and hedges | In hot, dry deserts
exercise: «Its bright red-orange berries in autumn are poisonous, so do not eat them.» What does poisonous mean? | Good for a snack | Sweet and tasty | *Harmful if eaten | Shiny and smooth
test: «The arum lily is common in gardens, and in Australia it can be a weed.» What does weed mean here? | A tasty herb for cooking | A flower grown for a prize | A tall tree with a hood | *A plant growing where people do not want it
test: «Some arums smell bad to attract flies.» Why would an arum smell bad? | To scare away all the birds | To make it rain | *To attract flies | To tell people it is ready to eat
exam: «It opens for only a day or two, and may not bloom again for years.» What does bloom mean here? | *To open its flower | To lose all its leaves | To grow fruit underground | To fall into water
